      {
         "id": "SECTION_introduction",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "<p><h3>Introduction</h3>Until now, we saw how our fingers helped us make different ways to count. But this was not always like this. For a long time, we had no way to show the idea of \"all fingers down\". In this text, we will look at a bit of the history of counting and how we got to the numbers we use today.</p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_history",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "<p><h3>The History of our Numbers</h3>The numbers we use today are old. We call them Hindu-Arabic numbers. They were first made in India. Then the Arabs learned about them, and they grew and spread around the world (<htcite0>).</p><p>We will mostly use Hindu-Arabic numbers. But we will not forget the Indigenous numbers we learned. Numbers can be shown in different ways, so we chose these to continue:</p>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "7b7d81af-c3ba-43fa-9bd8-06662ee66d1f",
                     "text": "Kunitzsch, Paul",
                     "page": "pp. 3 - 21",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2003",
                        "month": "05",
                        "day": "19"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": [
                  {
                     "type": "gregory",
                     "year": "2003",
                     "month": "-1",
                     "day": "-1"
                  }
               ],
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<table class=\"tawapukllay\" id=\"tblHandsCounting\"><tr><th>Left hand</th><th>Right hand</th><th>Numbers</th></tr> </table><p class=\"desc\"><b>Table 1</b>: Hindu-Arabic numbers and their relationship with our fingers.</p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><center><div class=\"first_steps_lab_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_lab question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-microscope\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_lab\"><b>Practice 1</b><br />If you have <b>Table 1</b> with you, color the numbers in it.<br /> Alternatively, copy them onto a piece of paper and color them as you wish.</p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_zero",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "<p><h3>Who represented the <b>0</b> first?</h3>We are showing the ten numbers with our hands. But things were not always like this. This knowledge was built over time. The philosopher Socrates saw these constant changes. He gave us an important lesson: <b>\"I know that I know nothing\"</b> (<htcite0> ; <htcite1>).</p><p><center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-brain\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\"><b>Reflection 1</b><br />Considering how little we know, what do you think about studying and finding answers to life's questions?</div></div></center></p><p>An example of this growth of knowledge is the first number in <b>Table 1</b>, the 0. It was one of the last numbers to be shown, even though we have had our fingers down for a long time. But who showed the number 0 first? If we start our answer with \"The first to show the 0 was....\", we should remember that our answer may change with new findings. Let's look at some examples.</p><p>Auwalu Musa and Anna Blume tell us that Indigenous peoples made different symbols to show fingers down. But they did not use them the way we do (<htcite2> ; <htcite3>). So we could say that the first to make and use a symbol were the Arabs (<htcite4> ; <htcite5>). On the other hand, when we study the work of Francisco B. Puente, we learn that Indigenous peoples in different parts of the American continent used different ways to show fingers down (<htcite6>).</p><p>From what we saw, we can say that different peoples showed fingers down in different ways.</p>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 0,
                     "uuid": "3b95809f-e017-4f2c-9c30-bc3db69ce7c5",
                     "text": "Plato",
                     "page": "pp. 15",
